  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of 'slow travel' has gained popularity in recent years, particularly among environmentally conscious tourists. This approach to travel involves staying in one place for an extended period, often weeks or even months, and immersing oneself in the local culture.\n\nIn contrast to traditional tourism, slow travel encourages visitors to engage with the community, learn about its customs and traditions, and participate in local activities. For instance, a traveler might take a cooking class to learn how to prepare traditional dishes or join a local festival to experience the vibrant atmosphere.\n\nProponents of slow travel argue that it not only benefits the environment but also provides a more authentic and enriching experience for the visitor. By slowing down their pace, travelers can appreciate the beauty of their surroundings, connect with locals, and develop a deeper understanding of the culture.\n\nHowever, critics point out that slow travel can be elitist, as it often requires a significant amount of time and financial resources. Furthermore, some argue that it can lead to cultural appropriation, where visitors adopt local customs without fully understanding their context or significance.\n\nDespite these concerns, many destinations are now embracing slow travel as a sustainable and responsible tourism model. By promoting this approach, they aim to reduce the environmental impact of mass tourism while providing tourists with a more meaningful and memorable experience.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What is the main idea of the article about slow travel?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It's a way to save money on transportation.\", \"It encourages visitors to stay in one place for an extended period.\", \"It's only suitable for rich tourists.\", \"It has no environmental benefits.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"b\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"What is a way that slow travel encourages visitors to engage with the local culture?\",\n      \"options\": [\"By staying in luxury hotels\", \"By joining local festivals and participating in traditional activities\", \"By only interacting with other tourists\", \"By ignoring local customs and traditions\"],\n      \"answer\": \"b\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"What is a potential drawback of slow travel, according to critics?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It's too expensive for most people.\", \"It leads to cultural appropriation.\", \"It has no environmental benefits.\", \"It's only suitable for young travelers.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"b\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What is the author's attitude towards slow travel?\",\n      \"options\": [\"They strongly oppose it due to its elitist nature.\", \"They are neutral and don't take a stance.\", \"They see it as a sustainable and responsible tourism model.\", \"They think it's only for environmentalists.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"c\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"What is the purpose of promoting slow travel in some destinations?\",\n      \"options\": [\"To increase tourist numbers\", \"To reduce the environmental impact of mass tourism\", \"To make tourists more reckless\", \"To encourage visitors to stay in luxury hotels\"],\n      \"answer\": \"b\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
